            <ArticleTitle>The attractiveness of opposites: agonists and antagonists.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/15360288.2014.1003680</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Opioid-induced bowel dysfunction, of which constipation is the most common aspect, is a major limiting factor in the use of opioids for pain management. The availability of an oral, long-acting formulation of oxycodone and naloxone represents a highly significant development in pain management. The combination of an opioid analgesic with an opioid antagonist offers reliable pain control with a significant reduction in the burden of opioid-induced constipation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
